+======================================================================+
|                 TERRAFORM META-ARGUMENTS                             |
+======================================================================+

WHAT IT IS
----------
A meta-argument is a special argument in Terraform
that controls HOW a resource behaves.

It does NOT configure the infrastructure itself.
It controls Terraform’s behavior.


KEY IDEA
--------
Normal argument  -> Configures the resource
Meta-argument    -> Configures Terraform behavior


COMMON META-ARGUMENTS
---------------------
- depends_on
- count
- for_each
- provider
- lifecycle


EXAMPLE 1: count
----------------

resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  count         = 3
  ami           = "ami-xxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.medium"
}

Result:
Terraform creates 3 EC2 instances.


EXAMPLE 2: depends_on
---------------------

resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.medium"

  depends_on = [aws_s3_bucket.logs]
}

Result:
Terraform ensures the S3 bucket is created first.


EXAMPLE 3: lifecycle
--------------------

resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.medium"

  lifecycle {
    prevent_destroy = true
  }
}

Result:
Terraform blocks destruction.


HOW TO IDENTIFY A META-ARGUMENT
-------------------------------
- Works in most resource types
- Controls provisioning logic
- Not specific to one provider


REAL-WORLD ANALOGY
------------------
Resource arguments  = What you build
Meta-arguments      = Rules for how construction happens


DEVOPS CONCEPT
--------------
Meta-arguments modify dependency graph,
resource iteration, lifecycle behavior,
and execution order.
